{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Research tools and techniques for debugging a GPU-side timing race that causes a wild out-of-bounds VRAM write on an AMD RX 7900 XTX (RDNA3, gfx1100). Context: the GPU is driven by tinygrad's own bare-metal \"AM\" driver (NOT the amdgpu kernel driver, NOT ROCm) over a USB link. A speculative-decode graph intermittently produces a shader store to a garbage address -> GCVM_L2_PROTECTION_FAULT (rw=1 write, client id \"cid=8\"), then the command processor (CP) hangs. The fault is a heisenbug: adding per-kernel GPU progress signals (extra serialization) makes it vanish, so instrumentation that changes GPU kernel timing hides it. We have: an FTDI debug port to the dock giving raw register/MMIO read+write (PCIe TLP reads) even when the CP is hung, and we control the dock firmware.\n\nResearch and report concretely (with specifics, register names, tool names, doc/source links):\n\n1. **SQTT (SQ Thread Trace) on RDNA3/gfx1100**: how it captures per-wave instruction traces to a VRAM buffer via GPU packets (minimal host perturbation). What registers configure it (SQ_THREAD_TRACE_*), how to locate the faulting wave/shader from a trace, and whether it can run without ROCm (just MMIO + a VRAM buffer). Is SQTT low-perturbation enough to not hide a timing race?\n\n2. **Post-hang GPU state inspection via raw MMIO** (the key non-perturbing method, since we can read registers over the debug port after the fault): which registers hold (a) the faulting wave's program counter and status — SQ_WAVE_PC_LO/HI, SQ_WAVE_STATUS, SQ_WAVE_EXEC, the SQ_IND_INDEX/SQ_IND_DATA wave-inspection path on RDNA3; (b) the VM fault details — GCVM_L2_PROTECTION_FAULT_ADDR/STATUS, and the client-ID (cid) decode table for the GC VM on gfx11 (what is cid=8?); (c) the CP state — CP_HQD_PQ_RPTR/WPTR, which dispatch/packet the CP stalled on. How do you halt/inspect waves on RDNA3 (SQ_CMD / SPI wave halt)?\n\n3. **umr (AMD userspace register debugger)**: can it read GPU state (waves, rings, VM faults) purely over MMIO/sysfs, and could it work against a device NOT bound to amdgpu (e.g. via a custom register-access backend)? What does `umr --waves` / ring dumps give for a hang?\n\n4. **rocgdb / ROCr wave trap handler (TBA/TMA, SQ_SHADER_TBA)**: how wave-level trap-based debugging works on RDNA3, and whether a trap handler could catch the bad store at the faulting instruction. Feasible without ROCm runtime?\n\n5. **General technique for \"instrumentation hides the race\" heisenbugs on GPUs**: how do people find missing GPU-side ordering / cross-queue (compute vs SDMA/DMA) hazards without perturbing timing? (e.g. reading ring pointers to identify the in-flight kernel, timestamp packets, decoding the command buffer at the stall point.)\n\nGive a prioritized, concrete plan of what to read and with what tool, favoring non-perturbing post-hang register inspection over live instrumentation. Cite sources."},"uuid":"8018356b-6a9e-497f-8484-2aadf5f1db66","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:23:42.836Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"8018356b-6a9e-497f-8484-2aadf5f1db66","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch"],"addedLines":["EnterWorktree","ExitWorktree","Monitor","NotebookEdit","SendMessage","TaskStop","WebFetch","WebSearch"],"removedNames":[],"wireHiddenNames":[],"readdedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6df583f7-792b-40fa-a876-b8760bab2955","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:23:42.849Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following deferred tools are now available via ToolSearch. Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them:\nEnterWorktree\nExitWorktree\nMonitor\nNotebookEdit\nSendMessage\nTaskStop\nWebFetch\nWebSearch\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"6df583f7-792b-40fa-a876-b8760bab2955","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- design: Create a design canvas - a multi-artboard visual design published as an Artifact that runs Claude Design's canvas editor (an early preview of Claude Design inside Claude Code). You DRAFT the design as .dc.html artboards laid out on one pan/zoom canvas; where saving is enabled for the user's account they refine every element visually (click-to-select, a properties panel, inline text editing, undo/redo) and Save publishes a new version for everyone, otherwise they get a view-and-export (PNG/PDF) preview of your draft. Good for UI mockups and screen flows, landing pages, marketing and social graphics, and print pieces - posters, flyers, brochures as single-page artboards; memos and reports as one flowing artboard. Use when someone wants a design, mockup, wireframe, UI or screen design, landing page, poster, flyer, brochure, banner, card, one-pager, or any visual layout they would rather tweak by hand than in code. Only for CREATING or re-seeding a canvas; an existing one is edited in its published Artifact.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch","skillCount":18,"isInitial":true,"names":["design","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","schedule","claude-api","workflow-authoring","claude-in-chrome","run","init","security-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"57b33e76-b1f6-48bc-a199-bd81349a5019","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:23:42.852Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- design: Create a design canvas - a multi-artboard visual design published as an Artifact that runs Claude Design's canvas editor (an early preview of Claude Design inside Claude Code). You DRAFT the design as .dc.html artboards laid out on one pan/zoom canvas; where saving is enabled for the user's account they refine every element visually (click-to-select, a properties panel, inline text editing, undo/redo) and Save publishes a new version for everyone, otherwise they get a view-and-export (PNG/PDF) preview of your draft. Good for UI mockups and screen flows, landing pages, marketing and social graphics, and print pieces - posters, flyers, brochures as single-page artboards; memos and reports as one flowing artboard. Use when someone wants a design, mockup, wireframe, UI or screen design, landing page, poster, flyer, brochure, banner, card, one-pager, or any visual layout they would rather tweak by hand than in code. Only for CREATING or re-seeding a canvas; an existing one is edited in its published Artifact.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, or handing the viewer a file to save. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- schedule: Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule. - When the user wants to schedule a recurring cloud agent, set up automated tasks, create a cron job for Claude Code, or manage their scheduled agents/routines. Also use when the user wants a one-time scheduled run (\"run this once at 3pm\", \"remind me to check X tomorrow\").\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"57b33e76-b1f6-48bc-a199-bd81349a5019","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/home/jacob/.claude/projects/-home-jacob-z-tinygrad/memory/dont-stop-when-hard.md","type":"AutoMemPinned","content":"Jacob repeatedly pushes back when I stop short on a hard problem. On 2026-09-04, mid-debug of a USB-AMD\nGPU \"device hang\" that gates the wide-speculative-verify throughput lever, I characterized the bug, judged\nthe remaining root-cause work too risky/hard to do autonomously, and wrote it up as \"best done with you\npresent.\" His response: **\"stop stopping when its 'hard'.\"** (This is the same steer as his earlier calling\nsingle-config timidity \"lazy\" — he has \"infinite compute\" and expects the hard work actually done.)\n\nThe durable lesson: when a task gets hard — a deep kernel/driver bug, an opaque hang, a multi-step\nroot-cause — the expectation is to **keep going and actually solve it**, not to stop and hand it back with a\nplan. Deferring hard work to \"when you're back\" or \"this benefits from your input\" reads to him as stopping\nshort, and he does not want it. Push through: build the instrumentation, read the actual fault, iterate to a\nfix. Only genuinely stop when truly blocked on something external (a missing file, a hardware capability we\ndon't have, an explicit decision only he can make) — and even then, exhaust the debugging first. Difficulty\nalone is never the reason to stop.\n\n**Do not ask for stopping points on long-horizon autonomous work.** On 2026-09-04, mid-build of the DFlash2\nparallel-drafter integration (goal: beat MTP decode throughput on the chestnut Qwen3.8-27B server), after I\nkept ending updates with \"want me to continue / do X or Y?\", Jacob said: **\"dont ask me if you should stop\nfor 2000 more hours... thats 83 days you have to work on this without asking me for a stopping point... now\ngo.\"** So for a big goal he has explicitly greenlit, keep iterating autonomously — build, test on the dock,\nread the result, fix, repeat — and report progress without pausing to ask whether to keep going. Save the\n\"want me to continue?\" question for genuine external blockers, not routine checkpoints. He treats the go-ahead\nas durable across the whole task, not per-step. (Related: sweep-broadly-when-compute-is-free — he calls\nsingle-config timidity \"lazy.\")"},{"path":"/home/jacob/.claude/projects/-home-jacob-z-tinygrad/memory/chestnut-serve-deploy-workflow.md","type":"AutoMemPinned","content":"On the XPS 9315 host (`j2`), the production tinygrad server (`tinygrad-server-chestnut.service`,\nserving Qwen3.8-27B on the tiny chestnut dock) deliberately does NOT run from the source checkout\nat `~/z/tinygrad`. It runs from a **frozen, non-editable pip install** in a plain venv at\n`~/z/tinygrad-serve` (`pip install ~/z/tinygrad`, no `-e`). Jacob chose this after a routine\n`git checkout master && git pull && git checkout <branch>` in the source repo invalidated the\nserver's LLM_CACHE (a ~9-minute cold compile) — and because a checkout of any branch lacking the\nlocal `USB_COPYIN_GUARD` data-corruption fix would have silently corrupted model weights while\nserving.\n\n**Deploying new server code is an explicit act, never a side effect of git.** The workflow:\n\n    /home/jacob/z/tinygrad-serve/bin/pip install --force-reinstall --no-deps /home/jacob/z/tinygrad\n    cd / && /home/jacob/z/tinygrad-serve/bin/python3 -c \"import tinygrad.llm.cli\"   # verify FIRST\n    sudo systemctl restart tinygrad-server-chestnut\n\nThe verify step is mandatory, not optional: the very first freeze silently dropped the\n`tinygrad.llm.kernels` subpackage (new directory, absent from pyproject's explicit package list\nand lacking an `__init__.py`) and the service crash-looped 22 times on ModuleNotFoundError.\nPackaging is fixed (commit `97cb1f4f2`), but any newly added subpackage repeats the failure mode\nunless pyproject's package list is updated -- so always import the entry module from a neutral\ncwd before restarting, and check pyproject when the fork grows a new directory.\n\nRun it from whatever commit has been tested; each deploy costs one ~9-minute cold compile\n(`MTP`/`AMD_GEMV` etc. are in the LLM_CACHE key), after which restarts are ~60 s warm starts.\n\nRelated constraints that keep this working:\n\n- The unit's `WorkingDirectory` must stay OUT of the source repo (it is `/home/jacob`): a repo\n  cwd shadows site-packages on `sys.path` and would silently run the checkout instead of the\n  installed copy, re-coupling production to git state.\n- The `.venv` inside `~/z/tinygrad` is an editable install for development and sweeps only.\n- The LLM cache is content-keyed (commit `e250c024f`) rather than mtime-keyed, so even dev-tree\n  restarts no longer pay for branch bounces — but the frozen install is still the production\n  isolation layer.\n- The workflow is also documented in comments inside `tinygrad-server-chestnut.service` itself."},{"path":"<auto-memory-index>","type":"AutoMem","content":"Memory files under `/home/jacob/.claude/projects/-home-jacob-z-tinygrad/memory/` (paths below are relative to it):\n\n- [reverify-bug-reproduces-before-fixing](reverify-bug-reproduces-before-fixing.md): Before deep-diving a fix for a known bug, re-verify it still reproduces on the current clean tree and consider it may be several distinct issues, not one.\n- [fix-bugs-dont-work-around-them](fix-bugs-dont-work-around-them.md): As stewards of the tinygrad fork with infinite compute, root-cause and fix bugs rather than routing around them with config workarounds.\n- [dont-stop-when-hard](dont-stop-when-hard.md): Jacob wants hard problems pushed through and root-caused, not deferred with 'this is best done with you present' — stopping short on difficulty is the failure he calls out.\n- [sweep-broadly-when-compute-is-free](sweep-broadly-when-compute-is-free.md): When exploring an optimization/tuning question and the box is free, run a full parameter sweep, not one cautious config — Jacob calls single-config timidity \"lazy.\"\n- [chestnut-box-performance-governor](chestnut-box-performance-governor.md): The chestnut serving box must stay pinned to the performance CPU governor permanently — it keeps drifting back to powersave.\n- [imatrix-calibration-must-be-diverse](imatrix-calibration-must-be-diverse.md): Don't calibrate our own imatrix-quantized weights on Hermes traffic — it's a single task/domain and overfits; use a broad, diverse calibration corpus.\n- [measure-concurrency-scaling-before-claiming](measure-concurrency-scaling-before-claiming.md): Measure the actual throughput-vs-workers curve before claiming a speedup; on the chestnut single-GPU server concurrency saturates early (knee ~3 workers).\n- [write-it-up-means-draft-only](write-it-up-means-draft-only.md): When Jacob asks to \"write up\" a reply, issue, or post, deliver the text itself — don't check tooling or attempt to file/post it anywhere.\n- [check-sweeps-index-before-proposing-experiments](check-sweeps-index-before-proposing-experiments.md): Before proposing a benchmark or tuning experiment in the tinygrad fork, check sweeps/README.md — prior sweeps and their conclusions are indexed there.\n- [port-concepts-into-tinygrad-fork](port-concepts-into-tinygrad-fork.md): Don't dismiss CUDA-only or incompatible community releases — evaluate their underlying techniques for reimplementation in the tinygrad fork or as self-made weights.\n- [chestnut-serve-deploy-workflow](chestnut-serve-deploy-workflow.md): The tinygrad server on j2 runs from a frozen pip install at ~/z/tinygrad-serve, never from the source checkout; deploys are explicit via force-reinstall + service restart.\n- [verify-distro-before-install-commands](verify-distro-before-install-commands.md): Read /etc/os-release before handing the user an install command — don't copy package-manager lines out of project setup docs, which go stale.\n- [bw-decomp-goal-standalone-linux-build](bw-decomp-goal-standalone-linux-build.md): In the bw-decomp project, OpenBW is a temporary oracle, not the target — the goal is a running native Linux build, which means porting past where OpenBW stops.\n- [reuse-original-benchmark-harness](reuse-original-benchmark-harness.md): When reproducing a prior benchmark, locate and run the original harness and prompt files verbatim instead of reimplementing them from a description.\n- [check-upstream-before-debugging-vendor-hw](check-upstream-before-debugging-vendor-hw.md): When tinygrad/tiny-corp hardware misbehaves, search upstream issues, X/Twitter and Discord for known bugs and firmware updates before deep root-cause debugging."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1b41f731-e498-4938-b256-2f65baad313d","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:23:42.858Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\n# Pinned memories (apply to every conversation)\n\n<pinned-memory path=\"/home/jacob/.claude/projects/-home-jacob-z-tinygrad/memory/dont-stop-when-hard.md\">\nJacob repeatedly pushes back when I stop short on a hard problem. On 2026-09-04, mid-debug of a USB-AMD\nGPU \"device hang\" that gates the wide-speculative-verify throughput lever, I characterized the bug, judged\nthe remaining root-cause work too risky/hard to do autonomously, and wrote it up as \"best done with you\npresent.\" His response: **\"stop stopping when its 'hard'.\"** (This is the same steer as his earlier calling\nsingle-config timidity \"lazy\" — he has \"infinite compute\" and expects the hard work actually done.)\n\nThe durable lesson: when a task gets hard — a deep kernel/driver bug, an opaque hang, a multi-step\nroot-cause — the expectation is to **keep going and actually solve it**, not to stop and hand it back with a\nplan. Deferring hard work to \"when you're back\" or \"this benefits from your input\" reads to him as stopping\nshort, and he does not want it. Push through: build the instrumentation, read the actual fault, iterate to a\nfix. Only genuinely stop when truly blocked on something external (a missing file, a hardware capability we\ndon't have, an explicit decision only he can make) — and even then, exhaust the debugging first. Difficulty\nalone is never the reason to stop.\n\n**Do not ask for stopping points on long-horizon autonomous work.** On 2026-09-04, mid-build of the DFlash2\nparallel-drafter integration (goal: beat MTP decode throughput on the chestnut Qwen3.8-27B server), after I\nkept ending updates with \"want me to continue / do X or Y?\", Jacob said: **\"dont ask me if you should stop\nfor 2000 more hours... thats 83 days you have to work on this without asking me for a stopping point... now\ngo.\"** So for a big goal he has explicitly greenlit, keep iterating autonomously — build, test on the dock,\nread the result, fix, repeat — and report progress without pausing to ask whether to keep going. Save the\n\"want me to continue?\" question for genuine external blockers, not routine checkpoints. He treats the go-ahead\nas durable across the whole task, not per-step. (Related: sweep-broadly-when-compute-is-free — he calls\nsingle-config timidity \"lazy.\")\n</pinned-memory>\n\n<pinned-memory path=\"/home/jacob/.claude/projects/-home-jacob-z-tinygrad/memory/chestnut-serve-deploy-workflow.md\">\nOn the XPS 9315 host (`j2`), the production tinygrad server (`tinygrad-server-chestnut.service`,\nserving Qwen3.8-27B on the tiny chestnut dock) deliberately does NOT run from the source checkout\nat `~/z/tinygrad`. It runs from a **frozen, non-editable pip install** in a plain venv at\n`~/z/tinygrad-serve` (`pip install ~/z/tinygrad`, no `-e`). Jacob chose this after a routine\n`git checkout master && git pull && git checkout <branch>` in the source repo invalidated the\nserver's LLM_CACHE (a ~9-minute cold compile) — and because a checkout of any branch lacking the\nlocal `USB_COPYIN_GUARD` data-corruption fix would have silently corrupted model weights while\nserving.\n\n**Deploying new server code is an explicit act, never a side effect of git.** The workflow:\n\n    /home/jacob/z/tinygrad-serve/bin/pip install --force-reinstall --no-deps /home/jacob/z/tinygrad\n    cd / && /home/jacob/z/tinygrad-serve/bin/python3 -c \"import tinygrad.llm.cli\"   # verify FIRST\n    sudo systemctl restart tinygrad-server-chestnut\n\nThe verify step is mandatory, not optional: the very first freeze silently dropped the\n`tinygrad.llm.kernels` subpackage (new directory, absent from pyproject's explicit package list\nand lacking an `__init__.py`) and the service crash-looped 22 times on ModuleNotFoundError.\nPackaging is fixed (commit `97cb1f4f2`), but any newly added subpackage repeats the failure mode\nunless pyproject's package list is updated -- so always import the entry module from a neutral\ncwd before restarting, and check pyproject when the fork grows a new directory.\n\nRun it from whatever commit has been tested; each deploy costs one ~9-minute cold compile\n(`MTP`/`AMD_GEMV` etc. are in the LLM_CACHE key), after which restarts are ~60 s warm starts.\n\nRelated constraints that keep this working:\n\n- The unit's `WorkingDirectory` must stay OUT of the source repo (it is `/home/jacob`): a repo\n  cwd shadows site-packages on `sys.path` and would silently run the checkout instead of the\n  installed copy, re-coupling production to git state.\n- The `.venv` inside `~/z/tinygrad` is an editable install for development and sweeps only.\n- The LLM cache is content-keyed (commit `e250c024f`) rather than mtime-keyed, so even dev-tree\n  restarts no longer pay for branch bounces — but the frozen install is still the production\n  isolation layer.\n- The workflow is also documented in comments inside `tinygrad-server-chestnut.service` itself.\n</pinned-memory>\n\nContents of <auto-memory-index> (user's auto-memory, persists across conversations):\n\nMemory files under `/home/jacob/.claude/projects/-home-jacob-z-tinygrad/memory/` (paths below are relative to it):\n\n- [reverify-bug-reproduces-before-fixing](reverify-bug-reproduces-before-fixing.md): Before deep-diving a fix for a known bug, re-verify it still reproduces on the current clean tree and consider it may be several distinct issues, not one.\n- [fix-bugs-dont-work-around-them](fix-bugs-dont-work-around-them.md): As stewards of the tinygrad fork with infinite compute, root-cause and fix bugs rather than routing around them with config workarounds.\n- [dont-stop-when-hard](dont-stop-when-hard.md): Jacob wants hard problems pushed through and root-caused, not deferred with 'this is best done with you present' — stopping short on difficulty is the failure he calls out.\n- [sweep-broadly-when-compute-is-free](sweep-broadly-when-compute-is-free.md): When exploring an optimization/tuning question and the box is free, run a full parameter sweep, not one cautious config — Jacob calls single-config timidity \"lazy.\"\n- [chestnut-box-performance-governor](chestnut-box-performance-governor.md): The chestnut serving box must stay pinned to the performance CPU governor permanently — it keeps drifting back to powersave.\n- [imatrix-calibration-must-be-diverse](imatrix-calibration-must-be-diverse.md): Don't calibrate our own imatrix-quantized weights on Hermes traffic — it's a single task/domain and overfits; use a broad, diverse calibration corpus.\n- [measure-concurrency-scaling-before-claiming](measure-concurrency-scaling-before-claiming.md): Measure the actual throughput-vs-workers curve before claiming a speedup; on the chestnut single-GPU server concurrency saturates early (knee ~3 workers).\n- [write-it-up-means-draft-only](write-it-up-means-draft-only.md): When Jacob asks to \"write up\" a reply, issue, or post, deliver the text itself — don't check tooling or attempt to file/post it anywhere.\n- [check-sweeps-index-before-proposing-experiments](check-sweeps-index-before-proposing-experiments.md): Before proposing a benchmark or tuning experiment in the tinygrad fork, check sweeps/README.md — prior sweeps and their conclusions are indexed there.\n- [port-concepts-into-tinygrad-fork](port-concepts-into-tinygrad-fork.md): Don't dismiss CUDA-only or incompatible community releases — evaluate their underlying techniques for reimplementation in the tinygrad fork or as self-made weights.\n- [chestnut-serve-deploy-workflow](chestnut-serve-deploy-workflow.md): The tinygrad server on j2 runs from a frozen pip install at ~/z/tinygrad-serve, never from the source checkout; deploys are explicit via force-reinstall + service restart.\n- [verify-distro-before-install-commands](verify-distro-before-install-commands.md): Read /etc/os-release before handing the user an install command — don't copy package-manager lines out of project setup docs, which go stale.\n- [bw-decomp-goal-standalone-linux-build](bw-decomp-goal-standalone-linux-build.md): In the bw-decomp project, OpenBW is a temporary oracle, not the target — the goal is a running native Linux build, which means porting past where OpenBW stops.\n- [reuse-original-benchmark-harness](reuse-original-benchmark-harness.md): When reproducing a prior benchmark, locate and run the original harness and prompt files verbatim instead of reimplementing them from a description.\n- [check-upstream-before-debugging-vendor-hw](check-upstream-before-debugging-vendor-hw.md): When tinygrad/tiny-corp hardware misbehaves, search upstream issues, X/Twitter and Discord for known bugs and firmware updates before deep root-cause debugging.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"1b41f731-e498-4938-b256-2f65baad313d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","attachment":{"type":"session_context","context":{"userEmail":"The user's email address is jakerosenthal@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.","gitStatus":"This is the git status at the start of the conversation. Note that this status is a snapshot in time, and will not update during the conversation.\n\nCurrent branch: amd-gemv-qwen38-jacob\n\nMain branch (you will usually use this for PRs): master\n\nGit user: Jacob Rosenthal\n\nStatus:\nM tinygrad/llm/amd_prefill.py\n M tinygrad/llm/dflash.py\n M tinygrad/llm/model.py\n M tinygrad/runtime/ops_amd.py\n M tinygrad/runtime/support/am/ip.py\n\nRecent commits:\ncdddd2188 llm: expose attn decode QT as ATTN_QT + document the LDS ceiling behind K<=3\nb15834867 llm: wire n-gram/prompt-lookup drafting into decode (NGRAM_DRAFT, default OFF)\nfcbb4831e llm: n-gram / prompt-lookup speculative draft proposer (host-side core)\n9c58a2f85 usb: drop the host-side copyin front-guard (firmware C450 fix handles corruption)\n0105fa1be usb: cap copyin SDMA ring at 4096 chunks — fixes large-copyin hang, drops safe-copyin"}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b71556a7-a8cb-4d2e-945e-f18f1eebc3dc","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:23:42.858Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# userEmail\nThe user's email address is jakerosenthal@gmail.com. Use it only to identify the user, such as for authorship, attribution, or filtering their own work. Never send it to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL, or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n# gitStatus\nThis is the git status at the start of the conversation. Note that this status is a snapshot in time, and will not update during the conversation.\n\nCurrent branch: amd-gemv-qwen38-jacob\n\nMain branch (you will usually use this for PRs): master\n\nGit user: Jacob Rosenthal\n\nStatus:\nM tinygrad/llm/amd_prefill.py\n M tinygrad/llm/dflash.py\n M tinygrad/llm/model.py\n M tinygrad/runtime/ops_amd.py\n M tinygrad/runtime/support/am/ip.py\n\nRecent commits:\ncdddd2188 llm: expose attn decode QT as ATTN_QT + document the LDS ceiling behind K<=3\nb15834867 llm: wire n-gram/prompt-lookup drafting into decode (NGRAM_DRAFT, default OFF)\nfcbb4831e llm: n-gram / prompt-lookup speculative draft proposer (host-side core)\n9c58a2f85 usb: drop the host-side copyin front-guard (firmware C450 fix handles corruption)\n0105fa1be usb: cap copyin SDMA ring at 4096 chunks — fixes large-copyin hang, drops safe-copyin\n\nIMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
