{"parentUuid":"24bb1484-c64e-4c3d-8c5a-e16ab5aefd14","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TRCJL2P1X9c8N3fSydPc2m","type":"tool_result","content":"I cannot answer your question based on the provided content. The webpage excerpt you shared contains only high-level documentation about ROCgdb's features and installation, such as that it \"Debugs ROCm applications running on AMD GPU-supported hardware\" and enables \"simultaneous GPU and CPU code debugging.\"\n\nThe content does not discuss wave-level debugging mechanics, trap handler implementation, register-level details like SQ_SHADER_TBA/TMA, or the underlying hardware mechanisms you're asking about.\n\nTo find this technical information, you would need to consult:\n- ROCgdb's detailed technical documentation beyond this overview\n- ROCdbgapi documentation\n- AMD GPU architecture specifications\n- ROCm/ROCr runtime source code documentation"}]},"uuid":"9144cafa-bffb-45bb-838a-66a11f62e26b","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:26:55.100Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":3270,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I cannot answer your question based on the provided content. The webpage excerpt you shared contains only high-level documentation about ROCgdb's features and installation, such as that it \"Debugs ROCm applications running on AMD GPU-supported hardware\" and enables \"simultaneous GPU and CPU code debugging.\"\n\nThe content does not discuss wave-level debugging mechanics, trap handler implementation, register-level details like SQ_SHADER_TBA/TMA, or the underlying hardware mechanisms you're asking about.\n\nTo find this technical information, you would need to consult:\n- ROCgdb's detailed technical documentation beyond this overview\n- ROCdbgapi documentation\n- AMD GPU architecture specifications\n- ROCm/ROCr runtime source code documentation","durationMs":2640,"url":"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/ROCgdb/en/latest/"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"24bb1484-c64e-4c3d-8c5a-e16ab5aefd14","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"b886e84f-352a-46ae-ba00-59a32751324c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HLBr3jSWiAdMV4EEza5nii","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"umr custom register access backend low-level access function without amdgpu sysfs debugfs raw mmio pci_bar\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"High-performance input-output devices supporting scalable virtualization\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11656916\"},{\"title\":\"umr: AMDGPU Userspace Register Debugger | Man Page | Commands | umr | ManKier\",\"url\":\"https://www.mankier.com/1/umr\"},{\"title\":\"C++ 17 Register Access - shincbm\",\"url\":\"https://www.shincbm.com/embedded/2020/08/30/cxx-reg-access.html\"},{\"title\":\"Handling memory-mapped input-output (MMIO) based instructions using fast access addresses\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9983893\"},{\"title\":\"Creating an ASIC Instance — UMR: User Mode Register Debugger documentation\",\"url\":\"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/create_asic.html\"},{\"title\":\"Reading and Writing Registers — UMR: User Mode Register Debugger documentation\",\"url\":\"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/libregister_access.html\"},{\"title\":\"GPU Memory Access — UMR: User Mode Register Debugger documentation\",\"url\":\"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/vm_decoding.html\"},{\"title\":\"User-Mode Memory Registration (UMR) - NVIDIA Docs\",\"url\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/networking/display/rdmacore50/user-mode+memory+registration+(umr)\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about UMR (User Mode Register Debugger) and custom register access:\n\nUMR is a tool to read and display, as well as write to AMDGPU device MMIO, PCIE, SMC, and DIDT registers via userspace. \n\nFor backend low-level access without relying on amdgpu sysfs or debugfs, the documentation shows that using PCI mode with 'use_pci' allows access to the device without a loaded kernel module, which provides direct MMIO access through PCI BAR (base address register) mapping.\n\nThe UMR library provides a structured approach to register access through its API. The type of register is indicated by 'type' which is one of: enum regclass { REG_MMIO, REG_DIDT, REG_SMC, REG_PCIE }, and the address is typically a dword address and certain access classes require byte addressing (such as REG_MMIO).\n\nTo access registers directly via PCI, you can specify PCI device coordinates (domain, bus, slot, function) and use the `use_pci` option to enable direct MMIO access through the PCI BAR without requiring kernel module debugging interfaces.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c1bbc638-eeea-41bd-981d-60d637d73fc9","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:26:55.997Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"umr custom register access backend low-level access function without amdgpu sysfs debugfs raw mmio pci_bar","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01YHNtnsmJJvgPyUaMsmDCZW","content":[{"title":"High-performance input-output devices supporting scalable virtualization","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11656916"},{"title":"umr: AMDGPU Userspace Register Debugger | Man Page | Commands | umr | ManKier","url":"https://www.mankier.com/1/umr"},{"title":"C++ 17 Register Access - shincbm","url":"https://www.shincbm.com/embedded/2020/08/30/cxx-reg-access.html"},{"title":"Handling memory-mapped input-output (MMIO) based instructions using fast access addresses","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9983893"},{"title":"Creating an ASIC Instance — UMR: User Mode Register Debugger documentation","url":"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/create_asic.html"},{"title":"Reading and Writing Registers — UMR: User Mode Register Debugger documentation","url":"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/libregister_access.html"},{"title":"GPU Memory Access — UMR: User Mode Register Debugger documentation","url":"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/vm_decoding.html"},{"title":"User-Mode Memory Registration (UMR) - NVIDIA Docs","url":"https://docs.nvidia.com/networking/display/rdmacore50/user-mode+memory+registration+(umr)"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about UMR (User Mode Register Debugger) and custom register access:\n\nUMR is a tool to read and display, as well as write to AMDGPU device MMIO, PCIE, SMC, and DIDT registers via userspace. \n\nFor backend low-level access without relying on amdgpu sysfs or debugfs, the documentation shows that using PCI mode with 'use_pci' allows access to the device without a loaded kernel module, which provides direct MMIO access through PCI BAR (base address register) mapping.\n\nThe UMR library provides a structured approach to register access through its API. The type of register is indicated by 'type' which is one of: enum regclass { REG_MMIO, REG_DIDT, REG_SMC, REG_PCIE }, and the address is typically a dword address and certain access classes require byte addressing (such as REG_MMIO).\n\nTo access registers directly via PCI, you can specify PCI device coordinates (domain, bus, slot, function) and use the `use_pci` option to enable direct MMIO access through the PCI BAR without requiring kernel module debugging interfaces."],"durationSeconds":5.960426851999946,"searchCount":1},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b886e84f-352a-46ae-ba00-59a32751324c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
