{"parentUuid":"b90de25a-498d-4ae9-8c70-b3e0c829244e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CemEu3MuyeeptCKb1Dj9r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"umr's `use_pci` mode (direct PCI BAR MMIO, no kernel module) and its ASIC-instance abstraction are key. Let me get the backend/ASIC-creation details and the wave-scan mechanics."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2591,"cache_read_input_tokens":65811,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2591,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CemEu2bHDd9miCng19KmQ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7cf10400-4a4d-428a-98cb-319c4eb9d687","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:04.656Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"7cf10400-4a4d-428a-98cb-319c4eb9d687","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CemEu3MuyeeptCKb1Dj9r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BfMURrkD7Ef48NfiqSXf6e","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/create_asic.html","prompt":"Explain how umr creates an ASIC instance and its register-access abstraction. What is the umr_asic struct's access_funcs / callback mechanism (mem_access, reg_read, reg_write, gpu_bus_to_cpu_address)? Can a user supply custom callbacks to read/write registers for a device not managed by amdgpu? Describe use_pci direct BAR mapping vs debugfs. List the function pointers a custom backend must implement."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2591,"cache_read_input_tokens":65811,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2591,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CemEu2bHDd9miCng19KmQ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bf84fbec-5dbe-4189-b536-dec11206e62a","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:07.589Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"bf84fbec-5dbe-4189-b536-dec11206e62a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CemEu3MuyeeptCKb1Dj9r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V2ygL1ndbYVrwSp1x3TEwu","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://www.mankier.com/1/umr","prompt":"List umr CLI options relevant to: (1) scanning/halting/resuming waves (--waves/-wa, -O halt_waves, disassemble at PC), (2) ring dumps and IB decoding (--ring/-R), (3) reading/writing registers (-r, -w), (4) accessing a device via direct PCI BAR without the kernel driver (use_pci, --pci), (5) VM/page-table decoding (--vm-decode). Quote the exact option syntax and what each prints for a hang (wave status, PC, SGPR/VGPR, ring rptr/wptr)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2591,"cache_read_input_tokens":65811,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2591,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CemEu2bHDd9miCng19KmQ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d8872473-8d8e-4cd2-b840-6c918724b755","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:12.091Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"bf84fbec-5dbe-4189-b536-dec11206e62a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BfMURrkD7Ef48NfiqSXf6e","type":"tool_result","content":"# UMR ASIC Instance Creation and Register Access\n\n## Instance Creation Overview\n\nUMR creates ASIC instances through `umr_discover_asic()`, which accepts a `umr_options` struct and an error callback. The resulting `struct umr_asic` contains family information, configuration, PCI mappings, and register database pointers.\n\n## Discovery Methods (Priority Order)\n\n1. **Virtual Devices**: If `dev_name` begins with '.', creates a memory-only instance (e.g., \".vega10\")\n2. **PCI Mapping**: Uses domain/bus/slot/func to locate hardware\n3. **Instance Number**: Selects GPU by index (default: 0)\n4. **Device ID**: When instance is negative, uses `forcedid` to find first matching device\n\n## Access Mechanisms\n\nThe documentation mentions two approaches but provides limited callback details:\n\n- **Debugfs Access** (default): Reads from `/sys/kernel/debug/dri/0/...` requiring a loaded kernel module\n- **PCI Direct Access**: Setting `use_pci = 1` enables direct MMIO via BAR mapping, useful for systems without kernel drivers (NTI projects)\n\n## Custom Callbacks\n\nThe page indicates custom accessors exist (\"plug in your own register, memory, wave, or ring accessors\") and references detailed documentation in the \"ASIC callbacks\" section, but **the specific function pointer signatures are not included in this content**. Implementation details require consulting the linked ASIC callbacks documentation."}]},"uuid":"058a7e75-ee02-4e2d-9b59-d8115e03e55e","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:13.577Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":8542,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# UMR ASIC Instance Creation and Register Access\n\n## Instance Creation Overview\n\nUMR creates ASIC instances through `umr_discover_asic()`, which accepts a `umr_options` struct and an error callback. The resulting `struct umr_asic` contains family information, configuration, PCI mappings, and register database pointers.\n\n## Discovery Methods (Priority Order)\n\n1. **Virtual Devices**: If `dev_name` begins with '.', creates a memory-only instance (e.g., \".vega10\")\n2. **PCI Mapping**: Uses domain/bus/slot/func to locate hardware\n3. **Instance Number**: Selects GPU by index (default: 0)\n4. **Device ID**: When instance is negative, uses `forcedid` to find first matching device\n\n## Access Mechanisms\n\nThe documentation mentions two approaches but provides limited callback details:\n\n- **Debugfs Access** (default): Reads from `/sys/kernel/debug/dri/0/...` requiring a loaded kernel module\n- **PCI Direct Access**: Setting `use_pci = 1` enables direct MMIO via BAR mapping, useful for systems without kernel drivers (NTI projects)\n\n## Custom Callbacks\n\nThe page indicates custom accessors exist (\"plug in your own register, memory, wave, or ring accessors\") and references detailed documentation in the \"ASIC callbacks\" section, but **the specific function pointer signatures are not included in this content**. Implementation details require consulting the linked ASIC callbacks documentation.","durationMs":4685,"url":"https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/create_asic.html"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bf84fbec-5dbe-4189-b536-dec11206e62a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"058a7e75-ee02-4e2d-9b59-d8115e03e55e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CemEu3MuyeeptCKb1Dj9r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DmPVutHHoMpT2CQhB1ZU5Y","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/ROCgdb/en/amd-staging/ROCgdb/gdb/doc/gdb/AMD-GPU.html","prompt":"How does ROCgdb debug AMD GPU waves on gfx11/RDNA3? Does it require the ROCm/ROCr runtime and amdgpu kernel driver (via ROCdbgapi and KFD)? Describe the trap handler / second-level trap handler (TBA/TMA) mechanism used to catch memory violations and exceptions at the faulting instruction. Can this work in a bare-metal driver context without ROCr/KFD?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2591,"cache_read_input_tokens":65811,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2591,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CemEu2bHDd9miCng19KmQ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e72c883c-7159-4cf6-88ea-b765e4f5bbf3","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:15.022Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"e72c883c-7159-4cf6-88ea-b765e4f5bbf3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DmPVutHHoMpT2CQhB1ZU5Y","type":"tool_result","content":"# ROCgdb AMD GPU Debugging Architecture\n\n## Runtime Dependencies\n\nBased on the documentation, ROCgdb **requires** the AMD ROCm runtime and amdgpu kernel driver for GPU debugging. The material explicitly states: \n\n> \"ROCGDB requires a compatible AMD ROCm runtime to be loaded in order to detect AMD GPU code objects and wavefronts.\"\n\nAdditionally, the system mandates compatible firmware and device drivers, with warnings issued if unsupported versions are detected.\n\n## Trap Handler Mechanism\n\nThe documentation provided does **not** contain specific technical details about trap handler implementations, TBA/TMA mechanisms, or how ROCgdb catches memory violations and exceptions at the faulting instruction level. \n\nThe closest reference discusses precision reporting:\n\n> \"the program location at which they are reported may be after the machine instruction that caused them\"\n\nThis indicates asynchronous exception reporting exists, but the material doesn't explain the underlying trap handler architecture.\n\n## Bare-Metal Feasibility\n\nThe documentation offers **no discussion** of bare-metal debugging contexts. The entire framework assumes ROCm runtime presence. Since ROCgdb fundamentally depends on this runtime for code object detection and wavefront management, adapting it for bare-metal scenarios would require substantial architectural changes outside the scope of this reference material.\n\n**In summary:** The provided documentation confirms ROCgdb's dependency on ROCm/KFD infrastructure but lacks the low-level trap handler details needed to address your bare-metal question."}]},"uuid":"8d3554a3-fc48-438e-9b87-9bd26f38c91f","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:21.267Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":50932,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ROCgdb AMD GPU Debugging Architecture\n\n## Runtime Dependencies\n\nBased on the documentation, ROCgdb **requires** the AMD ROCm runtime and amdgpu kernel driver for GPU debugging. The material explicitly states: \n\n> \"ROCGDB requires a compatible AMD ROCm runtime to be loaded in order to detect AMD GPU code objects and wavefronts.\"\n\nAdditionally, the system mandates compatible firmware and device drivers, with warnings issued if unsupported versions are detected.\n\n## Trap Handler Mechanism\n\nThe documentation provided does **not** contain specific technical details about trap handler implementations, TBA/TMA mechanisms, or how ROCgdb catches memory violations and exceptions at the faulting instruction level. \n\nThe closest reference discusses precision reporting:\n\n> \"the program location at which they are reported may be after the machine instruction that caused them\"\n\nThis indicates asynchronous exception reporting exists, but the material doesn't explain the underlying trap handler architecture.\n\n## Bare-Metal Feasibility\n\nThe documentation offers **no discussion** of bare-metal debugging contexts. The entire framework assumes ROCm runtime presence. Since ROCgdb fundamentally depends on this runtime for code object detection and wavefront management, adapting it for bare-metal scenarios would require substantial architectural changes outside the scope of this reference material.\n\n**In summary:** The provided documentation confirms ROCgdb's dependency on ROCm/KFD infrastructure but lacks the low-level trap handler details needed to address your bare-metal question.","durationMs":4846,"url":"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/ROCgdb/en/amd-staging/ROCgdb/gdb/doc/gdb/AMD-GPU.html"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e72c883c-7159-4cf6-88ea-b765e4f5bbf3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"d8872473-8d8e-4cd2-b840-6c918724b755","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V2ygL1ndbYVrwSp1x3TEwu","type":"tool_result","content":"# UMR CLI Options for GPU Debugging\n\n## (1) Wave Scanning/Halting/Resuming\n\n**`--waves, -wa [none | uq | ring_name | vmid@addr.size]`**\nDisplays active CU wave information including status and program counter. Outputs wave bitfields when combined with `-O bits`.\n\n**`-O halt_waves`**\n\"Halt/resume all waves while reading wave status.\" Enables safe wave inspection without GPU hangs.\n\n**`-O disasm_early_term`**\nTerminates shader disassembly at first s_endpgm instruction, supporting older shader formats.\n\n**`-O disasm_anyways`**\n\"Enable shader disassembly in --waves even if the rings aren't halted.\"\n\n---\n\n## (2) Ring Dumps & IB Decoding\n\n**`--ring-stream, -RS <string>[range]`**\nReads ring contents (e.g., `-RS gfx`). Displays rptr/wptr and decoded PM4 packets. Range format: `[start:end]` or `[.:.]` for current pointers.\n\n**`--dump-ib, -di [vmid@]address length [pm]`**\n\"Dump an IB packet at an address with an optional VMID.\" Defaults to PM4 decoding; supports SDMA (3), MES (2), VPE (1), UMSCH (5), HSA (6), VCN (7-8).\n\n---\n\n## (3) Register Read/Write\n\n**`--read, -r <string>`**\nReads register values by path (e.g., `*.vcn10.ADDR*`). Wildcards enable batch operations.\n\n**`--write, -w <string> <number>`**\n\"Write a value specified in hex to a register\" using complete path format like `fiji.uvd6.mmUVD_CGC_GATE`.\n\n---\n\n## (4) Direct PCI BAR Access (No Kernel Driver)\n\n**`-O use_pci`**\n\"Enable PCI access for MMIO instead of using debugfs.\" Useful when KMD is hung.\n\n**`--pci <device>`**\nSpecifies PCI device via domain:bus:slot.function format for direct hardware access.\n\n---\n\n## (5) VM/Page-Table Decoding\n\n**`--vm-decode, -vm vmid@<address> <num_of_pages>`**\n\"Decode page mappings at a specified address (in hex) from the VMID specified.\" Automatically enables verbose output."}]},"uuid":"468a1e47-9cd1-4de5-bff6-05ed883cee02","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:21.662Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":29417,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# UMR CLI Options for GPU Debugging\n\n## (1) Wave Scanning/Halting/Resuming\n\n**`--waves, -wa [none | uq | ring_name | vmid@addr.size]`**\nDisplays active CU wave information including status and program counter. Outputs wave bitfields when combined with `-O bits`.\n\n**`-O halt_waves`**\n\"Halt/resume all waves while reading wave status.\" Enables safe wave inspection without GPU hangs.\n\n**`-O disasm_early_term`**\nTerminates shader disassembly at first s_endpgm instruction, supporting older shader formats.\n\n**`-O disasm_anyways`**\n\"Enable shader disassembly in --waves even if the rings aren't halted.\"\n\n---\n\n## (2) Ring Dumps & IB Decoding\n\n**`--ring-stream, -RS <string>[range]`**\nReads ring contents (e.g., `-RS gfx`). Displays rptr/wptr and decoded PM4 packets. Range format: `[start:end]` or `[.:.]` for current pointers.\n\n**`--dump-ib, -di [vmid@]address length [pm]`**\n\"Dump an IB packet at an address with an optional VMID.\" Defaults to PM4 decoding; supports SDMA (3), MES (2), VPE (1), UMSCH (5), HSA (6), VCN (7-8).\n\n---\n\n## (3) Register Read/Write\n\n**`--read, -r <string>`**\nReads register values by path (e.g., `*.vcn10.ADDR*`). Wildcards enable batch operations.\n\n**`--write, -w <string> <number>`**\n\"Write a value specified in hex to a register\" using complete path format like `fiji.uvd6.mmUVD_CGC_GATE`.\n\n---\n\n## (4) Direct PCI BAR Access (No Kernel Driver)\n\n**`-O use_pci`**\n\"Enable PCI access for MMIO instead of using debugfs.\" Useful when KMD is hung.\n\n**`--pci <device>`**\nSpecifies PCI device via domain:bus:slot.function format for direct hardware access.\n\n---\n\n## (5) VM/Page-Table Decoding\n\n**`--vm-decode, -vm vmid@<address> <num_of_pages>`**\n\"Decode page mappings at a specified address (in hex) from the VMID specified.\" Automatically enables verbose output.","durationMs":8177,"url":"https://www.mankier.com/1/umr"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d8872473-8d8e-4cd2-b840-6c918724b755","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
