{"parentUuid":"45f5c6b6-c8d4-446e-9d67-e18344cb2072","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A4WzX2FH5jTxXskqi7qyjf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"amdgpu SQ_CMD wave halt CMD=SETHALT SQ_IND_INDEX GRBM_GFX_INDEX select_se_sh gfx11 disable_gws halt waves umr\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"AMD GPU (Debugging with ROCGDB) - ROCm Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/ROCgdb/en/docs-6.3.3/ROCgdb/gdb/doc/gdb/AMD-GPU.html\"},{\"title\":\"linux/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c at master · torvalds/linux\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c\"},{\"title\":\"[PATCH 6/7] drm/amdgpu/gfx11: enable wave kill for compute queues\",\"url\":\"https://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg109662.html\"},{\"title\":\"drm/amdgpu AMDgpu driver — The Linux Kernel documentation\",\"url\":\"https://dri.freedesktop.org/docs/drm/gpu/amdgpu.html\"},{\"title\":\"User Guide for AMDGPU Backend - LLVM\",\"url\":\"https://llvm.org/docs/AMDGPUUsage.html\"},{\"title\":\"Syntax of GFX11 Instructions — LLVM 23.0.0git documentation\",\"url\":\"https://llvm.org/docs/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAsmGFX11.html\"},{\"title\":\"[PATCH 7/8] drm/amdgpu: rework gfx11 queue reset\",\"url\":\"https://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg123355.html\"},{\"title\":\"[SOLVED] [amdgpu]] *ERROR* ring gfx timeout, but soft recovered / Kernel & Hardware / Arch Linux Forums\",\"url\":\"https://bbs.archlinux.org/viewtopic.php?id=288107\"},{\"title\":\"fn.s sethalt\",\"url\":\"https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/core/arch/amdgpu/fn.s_sethalt.html\"}]\n\nI found search results related to AMDGPU wave halt operations for gfx11. Here's what the search results indicate:\n\nWhen a wavefront is in the halt state, it executes no further instructions. The search results include references to gfx11 architecture-specific code and wave management functionality.\n\nSpecifically, the results show:\n\n1. **Wavefront Halt States**: Wavefronts can be halted by executing an S_SETHALT 1 instruction or using the 'set $status' command to change the bit in the AMD GPU wavefront's register that controls the halt state.\n\n2. **GFX11 Support**: The Linux kernel driver includes functions for GFX11 operations, including wave indirect register access via SQ_IND_INDEX and SQ_IND_DATA registers.\n\n3. **Wave Kill for Compute**: Recent patches to the amdgpu driver enable wave kill for compute queues in GFX11, with the wave kill functionality working the same for compute as well as graphics.\n\nThe search results contain source code references from the Linux kernel driver (`drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c`) that handle wave register indirect access and control, which directly relates to the SQ_CMD, SQ_IND_INDEX, and GRBM_GFX_INDEX registers you mentioned in your query. However, the specific CMD=SETHALT command and parameters you listed would require examining the actual driver documentation or source code directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"54dbe2ce-a6a0-4f9f-afa2-cdcddfc48186","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:50.147Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"amdgpu SQ_CMD wave halt CMD=SETHALT SQ_IND_INDEX GRBM_GFX_INDEX select_se_sh gfx11 disable_gws halt waves umr","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014KRAQv3SkDVS9BniZJtos8","content":[{"title":"AMD GPU (Debugging with ROCGDB) - ROCm Documentation","url":"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/ROCgdb/en/docs-6.3.3/ROCgdb/gdb/doc/gdb/AMD-GPU.html"},{"title":"linux/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c at master · torvalds/linux","url":"https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c"},{"title":"[PATCH 6/7] drm/amdgpu/gfx11: enable wave kill for compute queues","url":"https://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg109662.html"},{"title":"drm/amdgpu AMDgpu driver — The Linux Kernel documentation","url":"https://dri.freedesktop.org/docs/drm/gpu/amdgpu.html"},{"title":"User Guide for AMDGPU Backend - LLVM","url":"https://llvm.org/docs/AMDGPUUsage.html"},{"title":"Syntax of GFX11 Instructions — LLVM 23.0.0git documentation","url":"https://llvm.org/docs/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAsmGFX11.html"},{"title":"[PATCH 7/8] drm/amdgpu: rework gfx11 queue reset","url":"https://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg123355.html"},{"title":"[SOLVED] [amdgpu]] *ERROR* ring gfx timeout, but soft recovered / Kernel & Hardware / Arch Linux Forums","url":"https://bbs.archlinux.org/viewtopic.php?id=288107"},{"title":"fn.s sethalt","url":"https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/core/arch/amdgpu/fn.s_sethalt.html"}]},"I found search results related to AMDGPU wave halt operations for gfx11. Here's what the search results indicate:\n\nWhen a wavefront is in the halt state, it executes no further instructions. The search results include references to gfx11 architecture-specific code and wave management functionality.\n\nSpecifically, the results show:\n\n1. **Wavefront Halt States**: Wavefronts can be halted by executing an S_SETHALT 1 instruction or using the 'set $status' command to change the bit in the AMD GPU wavefront's register that controls the halt state.\n\n2. **GFX11 Support**: The Linux kernel driver includes functions for GFX11 operations, including wave indirect register access via SQ_IND_INDEX and SQ_IND_DATA registers.\n\n3. **Wave Kill for Compute**: Recent patches to the amdgpu driver enable wave kill for compute queues in GFX11, with the wave kill functionality working the same for compute as well as graphics.\n\nThe search results contain source code references from the Linux kernel driver (`drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c`) that handle wave register indirect access and control, which directly relates to the SQ_CMD, SQ_IND_INDEX, and GRBM_GFX_INDEX registers you mentioned in your query. However, the specific CMD=SETHALT command and parameters you listed would require examining the actual driver documentation or source code directly."],"durationSeconds":6.538935494000092,"searchCount":1},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"45f5c6b6-c8d4-446e-9d67-e18344cb2072","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"b9dcf3a6-4165-41b9-8bf2-975a536f87ca","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014hEqvWdom3xFCtViroaC6F","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SQTT thread trace perturbation sq_stall_en spi_stall_en stalls shader RGP overhead timing accuracy mesa ac_sqtt\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"RADV SQTT Profiling Support Expanded To Support GFX10/Navi - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/news/RADV-SQTT-Navi-GFX10-Support\"},{\"title\":\"Embedding stall and event trace profiling data in the timing stream\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9798645\"},{\"title\":\"LEO: Tracing GPU Stall Root Causes via Cross-Vendor Backward Slicing\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.20032\"},{\"title\":\"Throttling shaders based on resource usage in a graphics pipeline\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11776085\"},{\"title\":\"Method and system for detection of thread stall\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11436013\"},{\"title\":\"Prioritizing stall/reason data into priority information stream over PC/data trace\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/8943369\"},{\"title\":\"SQ Thread Trace (SQTT) workflow with AQLprofile — AQLprofile 1.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/aqlprofile/en/develop/examples/sqtt-workflow.html\"},{\"title\":\"Overview Windows | GPUOpen Manuals\",\"url\":\"https://gpuopen.com/manuals/rgp_manual/rgp_manual-overview_windows/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about SQTT (SQ Thread Trace) and related profiling concepts:\n\n## SQTT Overview\n\nThe SQTT block is used for performance profiling and exposes per-draw/per-dispatch metrics to help in profiling. SQTT support is found on AMD GPUs and has been extended through the Mesa Radeon Vulkan driver.\n\n## RGP and Profiling Overhead\n\nThe profiling overhead shows the amount of SQTT data that was written to video memory by the hardware while gathering the RGP profile, expressed in terms of memory bandwidth used to write the SQTT data.\n\n## Stall Tracing\n\nThe search results contain information about stall trace profiling in general. Both timing information and profiling data are effectively packed in the timing stream, and contributing stalls less than the threshold are filtered out and not reported in the timing stream.\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific technical details about the perturbation effects of `sq_stall_en` and `spi_stall_en` register flags, or detailed discussion of how enabling these stall counters affects timing accuracy and overhead in the context of SQTT profiling. For more specific technical documentation on these register fields and their impact on measurement accuracy, you may want to consult AMD's official GPU documentation or Mesa driver source code.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c69dbabd-3e25-4394-b54e-4e589c065471","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:52.368Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"SQTT thread trace perturbation sq_stall_en spi_stall_en stalls shader RGP overhead timing accuracy mesa ac_sqtt","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_011PQSUR92x3fbAtaGiv7P4C","content":[{"title":"RADV SQTT Profiling Support Expanded To Support GFX10/Navi - Phoronix","url":"https://www.phoronix.com/news/RADV-SQTT-Navi-GFX10-Support"},{"title":"Embedding stall and event trace profiling data in the timing stream","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9798645"},{"title":"LEO: Tracing GPU Stall Root Causes via Cross-Vendor Backward Slicing","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.20032"},{"title":"Throttling shaders based on resource usage in a graphics pipeline","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11776085"},{"title":"Method and system for detection of thread stall","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11436013"},{"title":"Prioritizing stall/reason data into priority information stream over PC/data trace","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/8943369"},{"title":"SQ Thread Trace (SQTT) workflow with AQLprofile — AQLprofile 1.0 documentation","url":"https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/aqlprofile/en/develop/examples/sqtt-workflow.html"},{"title":"Overview Windows | GPUOpen Manuals","url":"https://gpuopen.com/manuals/rgp_manual/rgp_manual-overview_windows/"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about SQTT (SQ Thread Trace) and related profiling concepts:\n\n## SQTT Overview\n\nThe SQTT block is used for performance profiling and exposes per-draw/per-dispatch metrics to help in profiling. SQTT support is found on AMD GPUs and has been extended through the Mesa Radeon Vulkan driver.\n\n## RGP and Profiling Overhead\n\nThe profiling overhead shows the amount of SQTT data that was written to video memory by the hardware while gathering the RGP profile, expressed in terms of memory bandwidth used to write the SQTT data.\n\n## Stall Tracing\n\nThe search results contain information about stall trace profiling in general. Both timing information and profiling data are effectively packed in the timing stream, and contributing stalls less than the threshold are filtered out and not reported in the timing stream.\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific technical details about the perturbation effects of `sq_stall_en` and `spi_stall_en` register flags, or detailed discussion of how enabling these stall counters affects timing accuracy and overhead in the context of SQTT profiling. For more specific technical documentation on these register fields and their impact on measurement accuracy, you may want to consult AMD's official GPU documentation or Mesa driver source code."],"durationSeconds":6.29572723199986,"searchCount":1},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b9dcf3a6-4165-41b9-8bf2-975a536f87ca","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"5858ef8e-cf84-4fcb-8dfd-eb84bda31eb9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e8587740-1817-4321-bc7c-520d0eac0a5b","agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YDJMDwqs7agdVJCwGBqGwb","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RDNA3 gfx11 trap handler TBA TMA SQ_SHADER_TBA_LO SQ_WAVE_TRAPSTS memory violation MEM_VIOL second level trap handler\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[RFC PATCH 2/2] drm/amdgpu: Implement kernel VMID trap handler for GFX10/11/12\",\"url\":\"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150573.html\"},{\"title\":\"[PATCH 1/3] drm/amdgpu: Add per-VM kernel queue first-level trap handler infrastructure\",\"url\":\"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150737.html\"},{\"title\":\"[RFC PATCH 0/2] drm/amdgpu: First-level trap handler for kernel queue VMIDs\",\"url\":\"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150564.html\"},{\"title\":\"[PATCH 0/3] drm/amdgpu: Second-level trap handler for kernel queues\",\"url\":\"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150734.html\"},{\"title\":\"[PATCH 2/3] drm/amdgpu: Implement kernel VMID SQ_SHADER_TBA/TMA programming for GFX10/11/12\",\"url\":\"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150736.html\"},{\"title\":\"amdgpu/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx8.asm at master · ROCm/amdgpu\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ROCm/amdgpu/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx8.asm\"},{\"title\":\"[PATCH] amdkfd: fix the cwsr trap handler for gfx11\",\"url\":\"https://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg102555.html\"},{\"title\":\"amdgpu/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx9.asm at master · ROCm/amdgpu\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ROCm/amdgpu/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx9.asm\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about RDNA3 (gfx11) trap handler configuration related to TBA, TMA, and second-level trap handlers:\n\n## Key Registers and Configuration\n\nThe SQ_SHADER_TBA_LO/HI and SQ_SHADER_TMA_LO/HI registers are written for kernel queue VMIDs using SRBM select.\n\nThe TBA address points to the device-level CWSR ISA BO (isa_bo), while the TMA address points to the device-level scratch BO (kq_tma_bo). TRAP_EN is set in TBA_HI to enable trap handling for each VMID.\n\n## GFX11 Register Format\n\nGFX11 uses reg-prefixed registers (as opposed to mm-prefixed for GFX10).\n\n## Second-Level Trap Handler\n\nThe root cause of missing second-level handler support was that the SQ_SHADER_TMA register for kernel queue VMIDs was never programmed by the driver, so there was nowhere to store the second-level handler address. The solution involves mapping a per-VM TMA buffer read-only into the GPU VM at a fixed address, and programming SQ_SHADER_TMA for all kernel queue VMIDs to point to this fixed address.\n\n## Memory Violation Handling\n\nWhile the search results mention trap handlers and wave state (SQ_WAVE_TRAPSTS), they don't contain specific information about how MEM_VIOL (memory violation) conditions are handled through the trap handler mechanism. The results focus on the infrastructure for configuring trap handler addresses rather than the specific exception handling details.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"52d76484-8baa-41f4-92ba-a6ce28734f9d","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:27:52.447Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"RDNA3 gfx11 trap handler TBA TMA SQ_SHADER_TBA_LO SQ_WAVE_TRAPSTS memory violation MEM_VIOL second level trap handler","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01NgwNSLe9n9Lbd7vrujTmNU","content":[{"title":"[RFC PATCH 2/2] drm/amdgpu: Implement kernel VMID trap handler for GFX10/11/12","url":"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150573.html"},{"title":"[PATCH 1/3] drm/amdgpu: Add per-VM kernel queue first-level trap handler infrastructure","url":"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150737.html"},{"title":"[RFC PATCH 0/2] drm/amdgpu: First-level trap handler for kernel queue VMIDs","url":"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150564.html"},{"title":"[PATCH 0/3] drm/amdgpu: Second-level trap handler for kernel queues","url":"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150734.html"},{"title":"[PATCH 2/3] drm/amdgpu: Implement kernel VMID SQ_SHADER_TBA/TMA programming for GFX10/11/12","url":"http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150736.html"},{"title":"amdgpu/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx8.asm at master · ROCm/amdgpu","url":"https://github.com/ROCm/amdgpu/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx8.asm"},{"title":"[PATCH] amdkfd: fix the cwsr trap handler for gfx11","url":"https://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg102555.html"},{"title":"amdgpu/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx9.asm at master · ROCm/amdgpu","url":"https://github.com/ROCm/amdgpu/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx9.asm"}]},"Based on the search results, here's information about RDNA3 (gfx11) trap handler configuration related to TBA, TMA, and second-level trap handlers:\n\n## Key Registers and Configuration\n\nThe SQ_SHADER_TBA_LO/HI and SQ_SHADER_TMA_LO/HI registers are written for kernel queue VMIDs using SRBM select.\n\nThe TBA address points to the device-level CWSR ISA BO (isa_bo), while the TMA address points to the device-level scratch BO (kq_tma_bo). TRAP_EN is set in TBA_HI to enable trap handling for each VMID.\n\n## GFX11 Register Format\n\nGFX11 uses reg-prefixed registers (as opposed to mm-prefixed for GFX10).\n\n## Second-Level Trap Handler\n\nThe root cause of missing second-level handler support was that the SQ_SHADER_TMA register for kernel queue VMIDs was never programmed by the driver, so there was nowhere to store the second-level handler address. The solution involves mapping a per-VM TMA buffer read-only into the GPU VM at a fixed address, and programming SQ_SHADER_TMA for all kernel queue VMIDs to point to this fixed address.\n\n## Memory Violation Handling\n\nWhile the search results mention trap handlers and wave state (SQ_WAVE_TRAPSTS), they don't contain specific information about how MEM_VIOL (memory violation) conditions are handled through the trap handler mechanism. The results focus on the infrastructure for configuring trap handler addresses rather than the specific exception handling details."],"durationSeconds":7.520398036000318,"searchCount":1},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5858ef8e-cf84-4fcb-8dfd-eb84bda31eb9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
