{"parentUuid":"e079bbab-44f2-4e9e-9588-27b27279e5a1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CemEzQrNXC7wwxarbYJ7f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1375,"cache_read_input_tokens":76914,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1375,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CemEzQ3kcXynHT9WSn5ek","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"345373c2-1577-4ca8-8a75-406c7b2bc1cd","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:28:27.421Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
{"parentUuid":"345373c2-1577-4ca8-8a75-406c7b2bc1cd","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a92286cd9de3a8ecc","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CemEzQrNXC7wwxarbYJ7f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive coverage. Let me write the report. I have all the concrete pieces: tinygrad's existing plumbing, the register offsets (already in autogen), the cid=8=TCP decode, and the tool/source landscape.\n\n---\n\n# Debugging the gfx1100 OOB-write timing race: tools, registers, and a prioritized plan\n\nThe single most important finding: **tinygrad's AM driver already contains ~80% of the non-perturbing infrastructure you need.** You have full raw MMIO read/write, a working SQTT capture+decoder, IH-ring parsing, and a post-fault `GCVM_L2_PROTECTION_FAULT_STATUS`/`_ADDR` decoder with a timing-neutral `FAULTMAP` that walks the allocator's block list. The gaps are (a) wave-PC readback via `SQ_IND_INDEX`, (b) a trap handler, and (c) reading the CP ring pointers at the stall. All are additive to code that already exists.\n\n**cid=8 decodes to TCP** (Texture Cache Pipe — the vector-memory L1 path). With `rw=1`, that means a **shader VMEM store** (`global_store`/`buffer_store`/`flat_store`/`scratch_store`) from a wave computed a garbage address. This is a shader-side address-generation bug, not a CP/SDMA descriptor bug — that narrows the whole hunt to the store's address operands (an SGPR base descriptor or VGPR offset that was read before a producer finished writing it). Source: `gfxhub_v3_0.c` `gfxhub_client_ids[]` (index 8 = `\"TCP\"`; 9/10 = SQC inst/data, 5 = CPC, 13/14 = SDMA0/1).\n\n---\n\n## What tinygrad already has (verified in-tree)\n\n| Capability | Location | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw MMIO reg read/write | `runtime/support/am/amdev.py` `rreg`/`wreg` (l.297–341) | Plus `indirect_rreg`/`indirect_wreg` (RSMU window) and `indirect_wreg_pcie` (PCIE_INDEX2/DATA2) — these last two are your **CP-hung** path and mirror what `umr -O use_pci` does |\n| VM-fault decode | `runtime/support/am/ip.py` `interrupt_handler` (l.502–524) | Reads `regGCVM_L2_PROTECTION_FAULT_STATUS` bitfields + `..._ADDR_LO32/HI32`, already prints cid/rw/walker/permission |\n| Timing-neutral fault→buffer map | same, `FAULTMAP` (l.506–522) | Walks `AMMemoryManager.va_allocator.blocks` **post-fault** — zero execution perturbation. Extend this, don't replace it |\n| IH ring parse (client/src/vmid/pasid) | `ip.py` l.477–494 | `SOC15_*_FROM_IH_ENTRY`; already classifies `UTCL2_FAULT`, `SQ_INTERRUPT_ID` |\n| SQ interrupt decode (MEMVIOL/ILLEGAL_INST) | `ip.py` l.496–501 | `err_type` table `['EDC_FUE','ILLEGAL_INST','MEMVIOL','EDC_FED']` — a MEMVIOL here is your smoking gun |\n| **Full SQTT capture** for gfx1100 | `runtime/ops_amd.py` l.208–330 (`sqtt_start`/`sqtt_stop`/`sqtt_config`) | Programs `SQ_THREAD_TRACE_BUF0_BASE/SIZE`, `_MASK`, `_TOKEN_MASK/2`, `_CTRL`, `COMPUTE_THREAD_TRACE_ENABLE`; per-SE VRAM buffers; `THREAD_TRACE_FINISH` event; copies `SQ_THREAD_TRACE_WPTR` back |\n| SQTT decoder + PC→instruction map | `renderer/amd/sqtt.py` (`decode`, `map_insts`, `wave_pc` tracking l.645–680) | Already reconstructs per-wave PC from `INST`/`INST_PC`/`ISSUE` tokens for RDNA3 |\n| CP HQD state | `regCP_HQD_PQ_RPTR`, `regCP_STAT`, `regGRBM_STATUS`, `regGRBM_STATUS2` present in autogen | See §2 |\n| Wave-inspect register offsets | `runtime/autogen/amd_gpu.py`: `regSQ_IND_INDEX=0x1118`, `regSQ_IND_DATA=0x1119`, `regSQ_CMD=0x111b`, `regSQ_SHADER_TBA_LO=0x09e6`, `regSQ_SHADER_TMA_LO=0x09e8`, `ixSQ_WAVE_STATUS=0x102`, `ixSQ_WAVE_PC_LO=0x108` | The constants exist; they are **not yet wired** into `regs.py`/AM helpers. This is the main thing to add |\n\n---\n\n## 1. SQTT on gfx1100 — and the perturbation caveat that matters for you\n\nSQTT streams per-wave tokens (WAVE_START/END, INST, INST_PC, ISSUE, VALU/VMEM, timestamps) to per-SE VRAM buffers, written by the SQ hardware block itself — no host round-trips during the dispatch. It's configured entirely by MMIO + a VRAM buffer, so it runs **without ROCm** (tinygrad already does exactly this). Registers involved (all already written in `ops_amd.py`): `SQ_THREAD_TRACE_BUF0_BASE_LO/HI`, `SQ_THREAD_TRACE_BUF0_SIZE`, `SQ_THREAD_TRACE_MASK` (SE/SA/WGP/SIMD select via `simd_sel`/`wgp_sel`/`sa_sel`), `SQ_THREAD_TRACE_TOKEN_MASK`/`TOKEN_MASK2` (which token/reg/inst classes), `SQ_THREAD_TRACE_CTRL`, `COMPUTE_THREAD_TRACE_ENABLE`, plus `GRBM_GFX_INDEX` to target each shader engine and the `THREAD_TRACE_FINISH` PM4 event.\n\nLocating the faulting wave from a trace: SQTT tags every token with SE/SA/WGP/SIMD/wave-slot; `renderer/amd/sqtt.py` already rebuilds each wave's PC. The wave whose last token is a VMEM store with no following WAVE_END (it hung) is your culprit, and `map_insts` maps that PC to the exact ISA instruction.\n\n**The caveat — SQTT is *not* guaranteed low-perturbation as tinygrad configures it today.** `sqtt_config`/`sqtt_start` set `spi_stall_en=1`, `sq_stall_en=1`, `reg_stall_en=1` (l.233–242). Those enable **backpressure stalls**: when a per-SE buffer hits its high-water mark the hardware stalls the SPI/SQ so no tokens are lost. That backpressure changes GPU timing — precisely the kind of perturbation that can hide your race (RGP even reports \"profiling overhead\" as the SQTT write-bandwidth for this reason). So:\n\n- For **repro-while-tracing**, add a low-perturbation SQTT mode: `sq_stall_en=0, spi_stall_en=0, reg_stall_en=0`, large per-SE buffers, and restrict to instruction tokens on a single SE (`SQTT_LIMIT_SE`/`SQTT_ITRACE_SE_MASK` already exist). You trade possible dropped tokens for near-native timing.\n- If even stall-free SQTT hides the bug, treat SQTT as a *post-mortem* aid only and lean on §2 (pure post-hang register reads, zero added GPU work).\n\nSources: [RADV GFX9 SQTT commit (mesa)](https://cgit.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/commit/?id=768d4f0551e38bd688e3e551d5276ceff5d2316b), [AQLprofile SQTT workflow](https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/aqlprofile/en/develop/examples/sqtt-workflow.html), [RGP overhead docs](https://gpuopen.com/manuals/rgp_manual/rgp_manual-overview_windows/), and your own `renderer/amd/sqtt.py` + `ops_amd.py`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Post-hang state via raw MMIO — the primary, zero-perturbation method\n\nBecause you can read registers over the FTDI/PCIe-TLP path *after* the CP hangs, this adds **zero** GPU-side work and cannot hide the race. Priority order to read once `is_err_state` trips:\n\n**(a) The VM fault itself** — you already read these; make sure to snapshot before clearing:\n- `regGCVM_L2_PROTECTION_FAULT_STATUS` → fields `CID` (8=TCP), `RW`, `MORE_FAULTS`, `WALKER_ERROR`, `PERMISSION_FAULTS`, `MAPPING_ERROR`, `VMID`, and the UTCL2 client sub-fields. Decode per `gfxhub_v3_0_print_l2_protection_fault_status`.\n- `regGCVM_L2_PROTECTION_FAULT_ADDR_HI32/LO32` → faulting VA (`<<12`). Feed to your existing `FAULTMAP`.\n- The IH entry (`ip.py` l.486–488) gives `vmid`, `pasid`, `client`, `ring_id` — confirms it's the GC hub / your compute VMID.\n\n**(b) The faulting wave's PC/status via the `SQ_IND_INDEX`/`SQ_IND_DATA` path** (this is the missing piece to add). The kernel's `wave_read_ind` (gfx_v11_0.c) is the template:\n```\nWREG32(regSQ_IND_INDEX, (wave<<WAVE_ID_SHIFT) | (ixREG<<INDEX_SHIFT))   # 0x1118\nval = RREG32(regSQ_IND_DATA)                                            # 0x1119\n```\nRead, per wave slot: `ixSQ_WAVE_STATUS` (0x102 — check `HALT`/`VALID`/`EXECUTE`), `ixSQ_WAVE_PC_LO`/`PC_HI` (0x108/0x109 — the exact ISA address it died on), `ixSQ_WAVE_EXEC_LO/HI`, `ixSQ_WAVE_TRAPSTS` (holds `MEM_VIOL`/`ILLEGAL_INST` bits — confirms the store faulted), `ixSQ_WAVE_HW_ID1/2` (which SE/SA/WGP/SIMD/wave the hang is on), `ixSQ_WAVE_IB_STS`, and if needed `SQIND_WAVE_SGPRS_OFFSET`/`VGPRS_OFFSET` to dump the store's address operands. **You must select the shader engine first** via `regGRBM_GFX_INDEX` (`se_index`/`sa_index`/`instance_index`, broadcast off — tinygrad's `set_grbm` at `ops_amd.py` l.91 already does this) and iterate all SE/SA/WGP. The PC you recover maps straight into your existing disassembly / `map_insts`.\n\n**(c) Halting waves so their state is stable while you read** — use `regSQ_CMD` (0x111b). umr's `-O halt_waves` does exactly this: issue `SQ_CMD` with `CMD=SETHALT` (broadcast across SE/SA via `GRBM_GFX_INDEX`) to freeze all waves, read their `SQ_WAVE_*`, then resume. If the CP is already hung the waves are effectively frozen anyway, but halting protects against a wave that's spinning rather than parked. (Model the encoding on `gfx_v11_0.c` and umr's `src/lib/sq_cmd_halt`.)\n\n**(d) CP state — which packet/dispatch it stalled on:**\n- `regCP_STAT` (already in autogen) + `regGRBM_STATUS`/`regGRBM_STATUS2` (`cpc_busy`, `cpg_busy`, `cpf_busy`, `tcp_busy`, `utcl2_busy`, `sdma_busy`, `*_rq_pending` — tells you *which* block is wedged; expect `tcp_busy`/`utcl2` set given cid=8).\n- `regCP_HQD_PQ_RPTR` (in autogen) vs the queue WPTR (add `CP_HQD_PQ_WPTR_LO/HI` — not yet in autogen) → the exact ring dword the CP stopped consuming. Subtract from your submitted PM4 stream to identify the in-flight dispatch packet. `CP_HQD_DEQUEUE_REQUEST`/`CP_HQD_ACTIVE` (used in `ip.py` l.427) confirm the queue is stuck.\n- The MEC/MQD regs already accessed around `ip.py` l.349–368 give you the doorbell/queue identity.\n\nBecause all of the above are reads of state that already exists post-fault, none of it perturbs the race. Sources: [gfx_v11_0.c `wave_read_ind`/`read_wave_data`](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c), [gfxhub_v3_0.c client-ID table](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfxhub_v3_0.c).\n\n---\n\n## 3. umr — usable, and its driverless mode is the closest analogue to your setup\n\numr reads/writes AMDGPU MMIO/PCIE/SMC registers from userspace, decodes rings/IBs, walks VM page tables, and scans/halts waves. Directly relevant options ([mankier umr(1)](https://www.mankier.com/1/umr), [ring/IB decoding](https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/ring.html)):\n- `--waves/-wa [uq|ring|vmid@addr.size]` + `-O halt_waves` — halts all waves, reads status+PC; `-O bits` prints wave bitfields; `-O disasm_anyways` disassembles at the wave PC.\n- `--ring-stream/-RS gfx` (or `sdma`) — prints rptr/wptr and decodes the PM4/SDMA packets in the ring: this is how you read \"which packet is the CP on\" if you don't want to hand-decode.\n- `--dump-ib/-di [vmid@]addr len` — decode the IB at the stall point.\n- `-r`/`-w` — read/write registers by name against umr's register database.\n- `--vm-decode/-vm vmid@addr npages` — walk the page tables for the faulting VA to prove it's unmapped/misprotected.\n- **`-O use_pci` + `--pci <domain:bus:slot.func>`** — direct BAR MMIO, **no kernel module**, \"useful when KMD is hung.\" ([create_asic docs](https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/create_asic.html))\n\n**Can it run against your USB device (not bound to amdgpu)?** Not out of the box — `use_pci` still needs the GPU to appear as a local PCI BAR, which yours does not (it's behind USB). But umr is explicitly built around a **pluggable ASIC/accessor abstraction**: `umr_discover_asic()` builds a `struct umr_asic` whose `access_funcs` (reg read/write, memory access, wave access, ring access, `gpu_bus_to_cpu_address`) are function pointers, and it supports **virtual devices** (`.gfx1100`-style, memory-only). Two practical uses:\n1. **Reuse umr offline**: capture register/VRAM dumps over your FTDI link, feed a virtual-device umr instance, and use its wave/ring/IB decoders and register DB rather than reimplementing them.\n2. **Custom backend**: implement umr's accessor callbacks to route `reg_read`/`reg_write`/`mem_read` over your USB/PCIe-TLP transport. That gives you `umr --waves -O halt_waves` and `-RS gfx` against the real hung device. This is the highest-leverage integration if you want a maintained wave/ring decoder; the callback surface is small (a handful of function pointers). See [umr libregister_access](https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/libregister_access.html) and [create_asic](https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/create_asic.html); source at [gitlab.freedesktop.org/tomstdenis/umr](https://cgit.freedesktop.org/amd/umr/log).\n\n---\n\n## 4. rocgdb / TBA-TMA trap handler — the way to catch the store *at* the faulting instruction\n\nrocgdb-the-tool depends on ROCr + KFD + ROCdbgapi (\"requires a compatible AMD ROCm runtime to detect wavefronts\"), so the *tool* is not usable on your bare-metal AM stack ([ROCgdb AMD-GPU docs](https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/ROCgdb/en/docs-6.3.3/ROCgdb/gdb/doc/gdb/AMD-GPU.html)). **But the underlying mechanism is pure MMIO + a shader BO and is fully portable to AM.** RDNA3 waves take a trap on exceptions (including MEM_VIOL) and jump to the **Trap Base Address**; you program it per-VMID via `SQ_SHADER_TBA_LO/HI` (0x09e6…) and `SQ_SHADER_TMA_LO/HI` (0x09e8…), setting `TRAP_EN` in `TBA_HI`. This is exactly what the current amd-gfx patch series *\"Implement kernel VMID SQ_SHADER_TBA/TMA programming for GFX10/11/12\"* does for **kernel queues without any userspace runtime** — strong evidence it works outside ROCr:\n- [PATCH 2/3: SQ_SHADER_TBA/TMA programming for GFX10/11/12](http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150736.html)\n- [PATCH series: first/second-level trap handler for kernel queue VMIDs](http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150734.html), [RFC](http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150573.html)\n\nFeasibility for you: write a minimal RDNA3 trap-handler shader (model on ROCm's [`cwsr_trap_handler_gfx9.asm`](https://github.com/ROCm/amdgpu/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx9.asm), gfx11 variant in the same tree), point `SQ_SHADER_TBA` at it, enable the mem-violation trap, and have the handler snapshot PC/EXEC/VGPRs (the store's address operands) into a VRAM scratch (TMA) buffer before halting. On the fault, the handler runs **inside the faulting wave at the faulting PC** — the most precise possible capture. The catch worth flagging: the trap handler is *itself* extra GPU code, so it can perturb timing like any instrumentation; keep it branch-light and only active when hunting. This is more work than §2 but is the definitive \"catch it in the act\" tool if post-hang PC readback proves the wave has already advanced past the store.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Beating \"instrumentation hides the race\" — the general playbook\n\nThe failure mode you hit (per-kernel progress signals serialize the GPU and hide the bug) means the race is a **missing GPU-side ordering/visibility guarantee** — almost certainly a producer→consumer hazard where a store's *address inputs* (an SGPR descriptor or a VGPR offset loaded from a buffer another kernel/queue just wrote) are consumed before the producer's write is globally visible. Since cid=8/rw=1 says the *address* was garbage, focus there. Techniques that don't add serialization:\n\n1. **Pure post-hang reads (§2)** — the gold standard. No added GPU work ⇒ cannot hide the race. Snapshot fault status + wave PC + CP rptr/ring, every time.\n2. **Timestamp packets instead of blocking signals.** Your current \"progress signal\" perturbs because it *waits*. Instead, have each kernel write a monotonically-increasing `RELEASE_MEM`/`COPY_DATA` **timestamp** to a ring of VRAM slots (fire-and-forget, no `WAIT_REG_MEM`). Post-hang, read the slots to see exactly which kernels completed and which was in flight — ordering visibility without back-pressure. tinygrad already emits `THREAD_TRACE_MARKER` and per-kernel markers; extend that pattern with non-waiting timestamps.\n3. **Ring-pointer forensics (§2d + umr `-RS`)** — read `CP_HQD_PQ_RPTR` vs WPTR and decode the PM4 at rptr to name the exact dispatch, then diff against the graph to find the missing dependency edge.\n4. **Cross-queue hazard hypothesis test:** the bug is timing-sensitive and you suspect compute-vs-SDMA/DMA ordering. Rather than adding a signal (which hides it), *reorder or fence one specific edge* you suspect and see if the fault rate changes — e.g., add an `acquire`/`release` cache op (`ACQUIRE_MEM` with the right GCR_CNTL invalidate bits) or an SDMA→compute fence on only the one buffer feeding the store's descriptor. If a targeted fence kills it, you've localized the missing hazard without global serialization.\n5. **SQTT with stalls off (§1)** as a middle ground when you need the instruction stream but can tolerate some dropped tokens.\n6. **Statistical localization:** with infinite compute, run the speculative-decode graph in a tight loop capturing only the FAULTMAP + wave PC on each fault (all post-hang). Across dozens of faults, if the faulting VA is always \"just past the end of buffer X\" or the wave PC is always the same store, the offending kernel and operand are pinned down deterministically even though each individual occurrence is a heisenbug. Your `FAULTMAP` already prints overrun-src/next-above blocks for exactly this.\n\n---\n\n## Prioritized concrete plan\n\n1. **Extend the post-hang dump you already have (highest value, zero perturbation).** In `ip.py`'s fault path, after the existing FAULTMAP, add wave-state readback: wire `regSQ_IND_INDEX`/`SQ_IND_DATA` (offsets already in `amd_gpu.py`) into an AM helper, loop over `GRBM_GFX_INDEX` SE/SA/WGP selections, and dump `ixSQ_WAVE_STATUS/PC_LO/PC_HI/EXEC/TRAPSTS/HW_ID1/HW_ID2` for every non-idle wave. Halt first with `SQ_CMD` (`CMD=SETHALT`, offset 0x111b). Read `CP_STAT`, `GRBM_STATUS`, `GRBM_STATUS2`, `CP_HQD_PQ_RPTR` (add `CP_HQD_PQ_WPTR_LO/HI`). Template: `gfx_v11_0.c` `wave_read_ind`/`gfx_v11_0_read_wave_data`. **This gives you the faulting instruction PC directly, next run.**\n2. **Map that PC to ISA** using the existing `renderer/amd/sqtt.py` disassembly/`map_insts` (you already build the pc→instruction map). Read the store's SGPR descriptor + VGPR offset operands (`SQIND_WAVE_SGPRS/VGPRS_OFFSET`) to see whether the base or the index is corrupt.\n3. **Loop-to-localize:** run the spec-decode graph repeatedly, logging FAULTMAP + wave PC per fault. Confirm whether the store, the buffer, and the \"past end of block X\" offset are stable.\n4. **Add non-waiting timestamp markers** (not blocking signals) per kernel so you can identify the in-flight dispatch and the immediately-preceding producer without serializing — directly tests the producer→consumer visibility hypothesis.\n5. **If the wave has already advanced past the store at hang time**, install a `SQ_SHADER_TBA` mem-violation trap handler (§4) to snapshot the operands *at* the faulting instruction. Model on ROCm's gfx11 cwsr asm and the amd-gfx kernel-VMID TBA/TMA patch series.\n6. **Optionally**, implement umr accessor callbacks over your USB transport to get `umr --waves -O halt_waves` / `-RS gfx` for free, or use a virtual-device umr on captured dumps.\n7. Keep SQTT as a **stall-free** capture for cross-checking the instruction stream, never as the primary (its default stall config in `ops_amd.py` perturbs timing).\n\n### Key files to read/modify\n- `/home/jacob/z/tinygrad/tinygrad/runtime/support/am/ip.py` (l.477–527 fault/IH path — extend here)\n- `/home/jacob/z/tinygrad/tinygrad/runtime/support/am/amdev.py` (l.297–341 `rreg`/`wreg`/indirect — add SQ_IND/SQ_CMD helpers here)\n- `/home/jacob/z/tinygrad/tinygrad/runtime/ops_amd.py` (l.91 `set_grbm`; l.208–330 SQTT — add stall-free mode)\n- `/home/jacob/z/tinygrad/tinygrad/renderer/amd/sqtt.py` (l.645–680 PC reconstruction / `map_insts` — reuse for PC→ISA)\n- `/home/jacob/z/tinygrad/tinygrad/runtime/autogen/amd_gpu.py` (`regSQ_IND_INDEX/DATA/CMD`, `regSQ_SHADER_TBA/TMA`, `ixSQ_WAVE_*` offsets — already present)\n\n### Sources\n- gfx11 client-ID table & fault decode: [gfxhub_v3_0.c](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/torvalds/linux/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfxhub_v3_0.c) (cid=8=TCP)\n- wave readback / SQ_IND_INDEX / GRBM select: [gfx_v11_0.c](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c)\n- umr: [intro](https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/intro.html), [waves/ring decoding](https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/ring.html), [create_asic](https://umr.readthedocs.io/en/main/create_asic.html), [man page](https://www.mankier.com/1/umr), [source](https://cgit.freedesktop.org/amd/umr/log)\n- trap handler TBA/TMA (bare-metal-relevant): [amd-gfx kernel-VMID TBA/TMA patch](http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150736.html), [series](http://www.mail-archive.com/amd-gfx@lists.freedesktop.org/msg150734.html), [gfx cwsr asm](https://github.com/ROCm/amdgpu/blob/master/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/cwsr_trap_handler_gfx9.asm)\n- rocgdb dependency on ROCr: [ROCgdb AMD-GPU docs](https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/ROCgdb/en/docs-6.3.3/ROCgdb/gdb/doc/gdb/AMD-GPU.html)\n- SQTT: [RADV/mesa SQTT](https://cgit.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/commit/?id=768d4f0551e38bd688e3e551d5276ceff5d2316b), [AQLprofile SQTT workflow](https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/aqlprofile/en/develop/examples/sqtt-workflow.html), [RGP overhead](https://gpuopen.com/manuals/rgp_manual/rgp_manual-overview_windows/)"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1375,"cache_read_input_tokens":76914,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1375,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CemEzQ3kcXynHT9WSn5ek","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5096ab3a-1fd8-440b-96ce-1f516b3975a2","timestamp":"2026-09-06T00:30:43.669Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/jacob/z/tinygrad","sessionId":"bbbf3962-a9cf-4c32-800a-58c69b1ea8de","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"amd-qwen-on-master","slug":"staged-whistling-hamster"}
